WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.460
In the video game industry, there's always
something else for gamers to play, and new

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.230
games risk losing their player-bases almost
as soon as they get them—a fact these games

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.320
found out the hard way.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.800
Here are the hotly anticipated games that
lost most of their playerbase in just a few

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:16.120
short months.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:18.710
Tom Clancy's The Division

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:22.930
Ubisoft's much anticipated third-person shooter
The Division launched to critical acclaim

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:24.730
in March 2016.

00:00:24.730 --> 00:00:30.150
By June—only three months later—the game
had lost 93 percent of its player-base, falling

00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:35.100
from 2.1 million players per day to a mere
143,000.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.129
While the online shooter was by no means a
bad game, The Division had its fair share

00:00:39.129 --> 00:00:40.679
of problems.

00:00:40.679 --> 00:00:45.440
Matchmaking issues and stat bugs stunted gameplay,
with cheating and hacking plaguing the Dark

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:46.440
Zone.

00:00:46.440 --> 00:00:49.920
These days, The Division is all but dead,
with less than three percent of the game's

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:52.129
launch-window peak still playing.

00:00:52.129 --> 00:00:56.399
It's tough to imagine Ubisoft feeling too
bad about the drop-off in players, however—seeing

00:00:56.399 --> 00:01:00.420
as they raked in $330 million in five days.

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.860
"Now to sleep the sleep of the just."

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:05.860
For Honor

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:11.509
Hitting shelves in February 2017, Ubisoft's
For Honor promised explosive player-versus-player

00:01:11.509 --> 00:01:16.530
combat, with gamers able to fight as either
knights, samurai, or vikings in a medieval

00:01:16.530 --> 00:01:17.530
setting.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:18.570
Sounds incredible, right?

00:01:18.570 --> 00:01:24.240
Like many games from the top-tier French developer,
For Honor looked—and in many ways, was—awesome.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:29.549
Unfortunately, severe technical issues rewarded
Ubisoft with a 95 percent reduction in the

00:01:29.549 --> 00:01:32.000
game's player-base in only three months.

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:36.530
In a game that's all about thinking ahead
and executing quick reactions, Ubisoft's decision

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:41.090
to run For Honor over a peer-to-peer connection
completely ruined the experience.

00:01:41.090 --> 00:01:45.290
Players who bought For Honor at launch were
seriously disappointed, and even organized

00:01:45.290 --> 00:01:46.990
a boycott of the game.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:52.759
Nobody wants to see a game fail, but—unfortunately—For
Honor just couldn't keep players interested.

00:01:52.759 --> 00:01:54.170
No Man's Sky

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:00.030
This game promised it all—some 18 quintillion
planets, nearly unlimited exploration, epic

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:02.090
battles, and space dinosaurs.

00:02:02.090 --> 00:02:03.240
So what went wrong?

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:07.689
Well, it turns out that the infinite reaches
of space can be kind of...boring.

00:02:07.689 --> 00:02:13.910
No Man's Sky peaked on launch day, grabbing
upwards of 212,000 concurrent players on Steam.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:18.620
Less than a week later, 78 percent of those
who gave Hello Games' space exploration title

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.530
a shot simply gave up.

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:25.540
Not even two full months later, No Man's Sky
was seeing less than 1,000 players per hour—resulting

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:30.030
in a 94 percent drop in only 42 days.

00:02:30.030 --> 00:02:31.800
Street Fighter V

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:36.830
Launched in February 2016, Street Fighter
V was supposed to be a major console exclusive

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:38.300
for PlayStation 4.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:43.060
Sadly, the vast majority of players who picked
up the famous fighting game tapped out—and

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.760
tapped out quickly.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.590
Plaguing the launch of Capcom's title was
the stark lack of content and no Arcade Mode—the

00:02:49.590 --> 00:02:52.270
bread and butter of the Street Fighter franchise.

00:02:52.270 --> 00:02:56.930
Even worse, rampant rage-quitting soured gamers'
experience in multiplayer matches.

00:02:56.930 --> 00:03:00.870
Quitters ultimately got what was coming to
them, but the early damage was done.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:06.020
The poor experience was enough to put over
75 percent of players off the game, and most

00:03:06.020 --> 00:03:08.780
people who left Street Fighter V never returned.

00:03:08.780 --> 00:03:11.040
"Game over!"

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.560
Resident Evil 7: Biohazard

00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:19.190
Admittedly, most people put down single-player-only
games after they've beaten the main story.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:24.530
Nevertheless, Resident Evil 7 saw 90 percent
of its playerbase disappear after only one

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.730
month on the market.

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:30.640
At the game's peak, it averaged roughly 15,000
players per hour on Steam.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:34.940
One month later, that number had dwindled
to 1,500 per hour.

00:03:34.940 --> 00:03:40.480
Rapidly declining player-base aside, Resident
Evil 7 was a pretty big success for the franchise,

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:45.470
with launch numbers doubling that of Resident
Evil 6, and with the game being received well

00:03:45.470 --> 00:03:47.540
by both critics and fans alike.

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:53.610
Still, at its best, Resident Evil 7 topped
out at #19 on Steam's charts, and shareholders

00:03:53.610 --> 00:03:55.540
apparently weren't impressed.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:59.940
Fortunately, none of that looks like it's
putting a damper on Resident Evil 8.

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:00.940
Evolve

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:06.710
It only took one month for 2K Games' multiplayer-centric
Evolve to feel the pressure of a rapidly-declining

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:07.710
player-base.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:12.480
Not only did the game receive mixed reviews,
but its expensive DLC package certainly didn't

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:14.790
leave a good taste in any gamers' mouths.

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:19.880
The game saw its player-base dwindle on almost
a daily basis, steadily decreasing until Turtle

00:04:19.880 --> 00:04:24.680
Rock Studios had no choice but to swallow
their pride and make the game free-to-play—a

00:04:24.680 --> 00:04:27.370
last resort for a game that released at full-retail.

00:04:27.370 --> 00:04:32.210
Making the game cost nothing effectively injected
some life into the title, but the boost didn't

00:04:32.210 --> 00:04:36.710
last long, and you'll find yourself sitting
in a matchmaking lobby for a long time these

00:04:36.710 --> 00:04:37.710
days.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:38.710
Battleborn

00:04:38.710 --> 00:04:43.040
Gearbox Software, the studio behind the popular
Borderlands series, poured more than two years

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:48.600
worth of resources into the creation of Battleborn,
only to have their first-person game completely

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:51.370
overshadowed by Blizzard's outstanding Overwatch.

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:57.380
Battleborn had such a rough launch, in fact,
that the game only saw an abysmal 12,101 concurrent

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:01.889
players on release day, and absolutely plummeted
in the days and weeks that followed.

00:05:01.889 --> 00:05:06.190
In a desperate attempt to keep the game alive,
the developers have made the game essentially

00:05:06.190 --> 00:05:07.190
free-to-play.

00:05:07.190 --> 00:05:09.620
Sometimes, the stars just don't align.

00:05:09.620 --> 00:05:11.490
Pokémon GO

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.350
Niantic's mobile app Pokémon GO took the
world by storm when it launched in July 2016.

00:05:16.350 --> 00:05:19.320
By September, however, the game was all but
abandoned.

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:26.460
According to reports, Pokémon GO peaked on
July 13th, 2016, with 28.5 million users in

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:28.230
the United States alone.

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.740
Nearly nine million of those users quit playing
by the end of the first month.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:35.860
By the following month, almost two-thirds
of the app's peak users decided they'd had

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.480
enough of the craze.

00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:41.760
Nevertheless, Niantic had a major hit on their
hands, proving that the Pokémon brand is

00:05:41.760 --> 00:05:43.450
as viable as ever.

00:05:43.450 --> 00:05:44.450
Titanfall

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:49.120
Released just a few months after the Xbox
One, Respawn's first iteration in the Titanfall

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:52.610
series was slated to be the first true "next-gen"
title.

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:58.010
Sadly, the game was hamstrung by content,
and saw its player-base dropped off quickly.

00:05:58.010 --> 00:06:01.310
The biggest problem was that Titanfall wasn't
just "an online shooter."

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:06.580
It was, for all intents and purposes, an online-only
first-person shooter—lacking a single-player

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:11.630
campaign, or any content for users not looking
to play the same multiplayer matches day in

00:06:11.630 --> 00:06:12.640
and day out.

00:06:12.640 --> 00:06:17.590
Plus, being an Xbox One exclusive severely
limited the player base to begin with, and

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:21.440
many of the console's gamers moved on quickly
to other titles.

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:22.940
